{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_gain",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "For supplied state v, the target mapping dv_dt is a quadratic odd drag law with a single shared gain wake_drag/mass: dv_dt = -g - (wake_drag/mass) v Abs(v), so deceleration magnitude grows exactly like |v|^2 at all admissible speeds.",
      "child_relation": "Relax the assumption that the effective resistive coefficient is speed-independent once the wake state is given, by allowing wake_drag to weaken with increasing |v| through a finite-speed saturation of drag crisis / slip-stream shielding. Then dv_dt versus v bends away from pure quadratic growth, with weaker-than-quadratic additional deceleration at high |v| while remaining oppositional and bounded.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "v",
        "wake_drag",
        "mass"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across trajectories that reach moderate versus large |v| while holding g, mass, drag_coefficient, and wake_relaxation_time fixed, the child predicts a flatter increase in downward acceleration magnitude at large speeds than the parent's single quadratic curve; one refitted parent gain cannot match both the near-terminal moderate-speed slope and the high-speed curvature simultaneously."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "speed_scale",
        "expression": "Abs(v)",
        "role": "instantaneous speed magnitude controlling nonlinear drag regime"
      },
      {
        "name": "drag_loading",
        "expression": "wake_drag*Abs(v)/mass",
        "role": "instantaneous drag-induced acceleration scale relative to inertia"
      },
      {
        "name": "gravity_drag_ratio",
        "expression": "wake_drag*v*Abs(v)/(mass*g)",
        "role": "relative importance of resistive and gravitational accelerations"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when trajectories sample a broad enough speed range that |v| transitions from low/moderate values, where the parent's quadratic law is approximately valid, to larger values where drag-coefficient weakening is non-negligible but still leaves resistance positive and the long-time speed finite.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, the mechanism appears as mild deviation from a single quadratic dv_dt-versus-v curve at the largest sampled |v|; in holdout with faster initial drops or stronger transient wake mismatch, the same speed-dependent coefficient produces a clearer flattening of acceleration growth rather than a new qualitative regime.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent by taking the speed-dependence scale to infinity or its amplitude to zero, so the effective drag coefficient becomes independent of |v| and the law returns to dv_dt = -g - (wake_drag/mass) v Abs(v).",
    "freedom_change": "add_state",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "The omitted physics is a delayed flow-regime adjustment distinct from the existing wake-strength relaxation: a separate state is needed if the speed-dependent drag reduction should develop over finite time rather than instantaneously, because the current states v and wake_drag cannot encode both wake magnitude and regime occupancy without collapsing back to an algebraic coefficient.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes that, once wake_drag is specified, resistive force remains exactly proportional to v|v| with a speed-independent coefficient over the entire admissible speed range.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The drag response is symmetric with respect to direction and grows quadratically with speed magnitude with one effective coefficient independent of speed regime.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "At sufficiently large Reynolds number or disturbed-flow intensity, the surrounding flow can transition toward a lower effective drag regime; this produces a smooth high-speed weakening of the drag coefficient, plausibly with finite relaxation rather than instant switching.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "v",
      "wake_drag",
      "mass",
      "wake_relaxation_time",
      "drag_coefficient"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the parent's exact quadratic drag-growth assumption, not to add an unrelated force. Vary the initial velocity so that some runs remain in moderate-speed descent while others reach substantially larger |v|; hold g, mass, drag_coefficient, and wake_relaxation_time fixed. The parent maps the observed state v to dv_dt through one shared quadratic gain wake_drag/mass, so after refitting its parameters it still predicts a single parabola in v for each wake_drag value. A child with speed-regime drag weakening changes the target mapping itself: at larger |v| the slope of dv_dt with respect to v and the curvature of dv_dt versus |v| both reduce relative to the parent, while at lower |v| it remains close to quadratic. This is observable only if the sampled trajectories actually explore a sufficiently wide speed range; otherwise the refinement may be weak and should be acknowledged as such. Because the contrast concerns the shape of dv_dt as a function of the supplied state v, not merely an unobserved auxiliary trajectory, it is a genuine target-function complexity increase. A separate new state is justified if that regime change is assumed to relax over time; otherwise any effect would be an instantaneous algebraic correction and could miss the documented lineage preference for finite-time wake development."
  },
